  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved partnerships, workers and vulnerable groups are effectively protected   from unacceptable forms of work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This CPO has 4 major objectives: 1. HIV/AIDS 2. OSH review 3. Disability Project: FJI/25/50/UND and 4. Child Labour.
1. HIV/AIDS: Fiji is facing a significant HIV outbreak and needs to align with ILO&#x2019;s HIV Workplace Policy Guidelines and Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) principles and the ILO&apos;s SOLVE toolkit. The response involves workplace policies for prevention, testing, and support, in collaboration with ILO and UNAIDS. Key interventions include policy development, education, stigma reduction, and improved access to treatment for workers and the community. Beneficiaries are the Fijian workers and their communities.
2. OSH Review: The ILO recognizes a safe working environment as a fundamental right. Fiji must review its Health and Safety at Work Act (HASAWA 1996) to align with international standards, protect workers, and promote a culture of prevention. This review will benefit workers, their families, and the wider community.
3. Disability FJI/25/50/UND: This programme aims to enhance the rights and opportunities of persons with disabilities in Fiji, who often experience poverty, unemployment, and stigma. It focuses on accountability, equality, CRPD-compliant budgeting, and inclusive service delivery, while integrating disability considerations into national humanitarian efforts, benefiting persons with disabilities and their communities.
4. Child Labour - To continue strengthening Fiji&#x2019;s national institutional framework and constituents&#x2019; capacities to address child labour based on coordinated action and stakeholder engagement and support Fiji as a Pathfinder country for Alliance 8.7 since 2018. Activities include finalize the National Child Labour Policy and endorse by 31 December 2025, and improve the National Action Plan outlining the National Roadmap under Alliance 8.7 by 31 Dec 2025. Beneficiaries will be the children of Fiji and public at large.</narrative>
  </description>
